#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Watch the upstream sources the WAF packages are built from (B323, B337).

    tools/upstream-watch check --source <tree> [--apply] [--security-only]
                               [--report <file>]
    tools/upstream-watch due --base <tree> --head <tree> [--vulnerable]
    tools/upstream-watch vuln --source <tree> --cache <dir>
    tools/upstream-watch ran --repository <owner/name> [--workflow <file> --hours <n>]
    tools/upstream-watch locks --source <tree> [--apply] [--report <file>]
    tools/upstream-watch dependabot --commits <file>

Four pins, all in the tree: the Coraza SPOA engine (a commit), the OWASP Core
Rule Set (an LTS release), the Go that compiles the engine, and the
govulncheck that reads it. `check` asks upstream what is newer, verifies what
it would pin - the rule set's signature against the key in keys/upstream/, the
Go archive's digest as go.dev publishes it, the engine's archive by the commit
its release tag names - and with `--apply` writes the new pins into the tree.

Each change has a class. A patch or minor change is merged by itself once the
upstream release is two days old (seven until 26-10-23, B354); a major one never
is - it is reported for a person and left out of the tree. `due` reads the change between two trees
fresh from upstream and answers whether the newer one may be merged now; with
`--vulnerable` (the pinned engine reaches a known vulnerability today) the wait
is two days. `vuln` runs govulncheck on the pinned engine as the build does, and asks OSV
about the SCEP client's pinned certmonger as its build does (B383); it exits 3
when either finds something. `ran` asks whether the nightly run still
happens: GitHub's schedule is best effort and drops a run without a trace, so a
watch that has stopped would otherwise say nothing at all.

`dependabot` reads a Dependabot pull request's commits (`gh api
repos/<r>/pulls/<n>/commits`) and answers whether the watch may merge it: every
commit Dependabot's and signed, every update a patch or minor one, as the
metadata block Dependabot writes into each commit message says (B354).

`locks` is the same watch over the Python locks (B353): `uv audit` on each of
`uv.lock`, `ui/uv.lock` and `certbot/uv.lock`, and with `--apply` a
`uv lock --upgrade-package` for every package an advisory names a fixed version
for. A package the upgrade does not clear - no fix yet, or one the project's
own constraints keep out - is reported as held, for a person. An advisory
failed every pull request until somebody moved the lock (PR #4, 26-10-23).

What counts as major, by component:
    coraza       0.x: a 0.y step (an 0.x minor may break); then semver
    crs          a new LTS line; releases outside the LTS line are not candidates
    go           a new Go line (1.27 after 1.26)
    govulncheck  semver
"""

